  <subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>
	
	<p>A capacitor is made up of two conductors separated by an insulator.</p>
	
	<definition xml:id="capacitance">
		<title>Capacitance</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>The <term>capacitance</term> of a capacitor is defined as <me>C = Q / \Delta V</me> and is dependent on the geometry of the capacitor. The units of capacitance are in farads (F). </p>
		</statement>
	</definition>

	<assumption xml:id="a_parallel-plate">
	<title>Parallel-plate Capacitor</title>
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>A simplified and broadly applicable model for many capacitors is to treat the conductors as two square parallel plates, shown in cross-section below, separated by a distance <m>d</m> that is much smaller than the width of the plates, which each have area <m>A</m>.  Using this assumption:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The charge on each plate is uniformly distributed.</alert>  One plate has charge <m>+Q</m> and the other has charge <m>-Q</m>.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The electric field between the plates is uniform,</alert> with magnitude <m>E = \frac{Q}{\epsilon_0 A}</m> pointing from the positive to the negative plate.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The capacitance depends on the geometry of the plates:</alert> <m>C = \frac{\epsilon_0 A}{d}</m>.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
			<figure xml:id="fig_PPCapacitor">
			  <title>Parallel Plate Capacitor Figure</title>
			<caption>Charge diagram and electric field for a parallel plate capacitor.</caption>
			<image source="PPCapacitor.png" width = "40%" ><description>A vertical plate of negative charge on the left at z = 0, a vertical plate of positive charge on the right at z= d, with a uniform leftward electric field in between.</description></image>
		</figure>
	</statement>
</assumption>


